<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Gabon (French colony)</h1><p class="st-meta">1843 CE – 1958 CE · capital Libreville</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">French rule over the Gabon estuary and its hinterland, from coastal treaty posts in the 1840s to an autonomous republic in 1958.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>French naval officers signed treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpongwe">Mpongwe</a> rulers of the Gabon estuary, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-rapontchombo">King Denis</a> Rapontchombo in 1839 and King Louis in 1842, and built a fort in 1843 to support anti-slave-trade patrols. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libreville">Libreville</a> was founded in 1849 by Africans freed from a captured slave ship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-savorgnan-de-brazza">Pierre Savorgnan de Brazza's</a> expeditions from the 1870s pushed claims inland.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Gabon was long administered with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-congo">French Congo</a> and from 1910 as part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a>. Concession companies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> cutting, especially of okoumé, and forced labour shaped the economy, while Catholic and Protestant missions spread schooling. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-schweitzer">Albert Schweitzer</a> ran his hospital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lambarene">Lambaréné</a> from 1913.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1958 Gabon became an autonomous republic within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a> and gained full independence on 17 August 1960 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-mba">Léon M'ba</a>, keeping close economic and military ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libreville">Libreville</a> as its capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1843 CE – 1958 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.38%;width:2.29%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1843 CE – 1958 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Libreville</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Antchouwé Kowe Rapontchombo, Pierre Savorgnan de Brazza, Léon M'ba</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Fang, Myene</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/gabon">Gabon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gabon, Comptoirs du Gabon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1900&amp;focus=gabon-french-colony" aria-label="Gabon (French colony) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4942 2162 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gabon (French colony) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5262 2418L5054 728M5262 2418L5262 2418M5262 2418L5424 2593"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M5262 2418h0M5424 2593h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5262 2418h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5262 2418h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Libreville. <a href="/atlas?year=1900&amp;focus=gabon-french-colony">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

